-- rules/nullary.rofl — WHICH RULES ARE PROPOSITIONS WEARING A SUBJECT?
--
-- The owner asked whether reflection can derive this rather than a person
-- reading bodies. IT CAN, ENTIRELY FROM THE PUBLIC SURFACE, and the first
-- draft of this file got that wrong in a way worth keeping.
--
-- THE FIRST DRAFT READ `premise_var` AND `slot_arity` and answered ZERO on
-- boot.rofl. The zero was meaningless: those two are seeded by the host into
-- the kernel's OWN private store for `safety.rofl`, and in an ordinary store
-- they are EMPTY — measured, 0 rows beside 29 rules and 29 `concludes`. A
-- model over an unpopulated relation returns a clean-looking negative, which
-- is the failure this repository has a word for.
--
-- `conclusion_lit` and `premise_lit` are RESERVED but READABLE, `$` IS
-- writable in a rule body (safety.rofl already matches `$builtin(_, _)`), and
-- the reified shape is `$lit(Rel, Persp, Args, Tense)` with `Args` a
-- `$cons`/`$nil` list and a variable reified as `$var("Name")`. Everything
-- this file needs is there. K IS 1-BASED: the first draft asked for K = 0 and
-- got nothing from that too.
--
-- A nullary relation is a PROPOSITION: arity n is a set of n-tuples, there is
-- exactly one 0-tuple, so the extension is empty or the singleton — a truth
-- value. This language has no nullary head, so a rule concluding a proposition
-- must dress it. This file finds the dresses.

-- ===================================================== WALKING AN ARGUMENT LIST
-- The reflection hands a list; Datalog walks it. Range-restricted at every
-- step: the tail is bound by the match that produced it.
head_args(R, Args)  :- conclusion_lit(R, 1, $lit(_, _, Args, _)).
body_args(R, K, Args) :- premise_lit(R, K, $lit(_, _, Args, _)).

head_cell(R, L)     :- head_args(R, L).
head_cell(R, T)     :- head_cell(R, $cons(_, T)).
head_var(R, V)      :- head_cell(R, $cons($var(V), _)).

body_cell(R, K, L)  :- body_args(R, K, L).
body_cell(R, K, T)  :- body_cell(R, K, $cons(_, T)).
body_var(R, K, V)   :- body_cell(R, K, $cons($var(V), _)).

-- THE PERSPECTIVE IS A SLOT AND THE FIRST THREE DRAFTS WALKED PAST IT. A
-- reified literal is `$lit(Rel, Persp, Args, Tense)` and every walk above reads
-- ARGS; a variable standing in the PERSPECTIVE is bound by the premise exactly
-- as an argument is, and it joins premises exactly as an argument does.
-- `mechanism_seen[audit](M) :- env_ran(E), resolve_via[E](_, M, _).` is the
-- case that measured it — the two premises are joined THROUGH the book, and
-- with the slot invisible they read as a cross product and `M` as a passenger.
-- That is the same defect as the `$not` blind spot and the `$builtin` one,
-- arriving a third time in a different slot of the same term.
persp_var(R, K, V)  :- premise_lit(R, K, $lit(_, $var(V), _, _)).
body_var(R, K, V)   :- persp_var(R, K, V).
head_var(R, V)      :- conclusion_lit(R, 1, $lit(_, $var(V), _, _)).

-- A NEGATED PREMISE BINDS NOTHING AND CONNECTS EVERYTHING, and the first draft
-- of this file got that backwards. It read only `$lit(...)`, wrote down that
-- ignoring `$not(...)` was `safe in this direction` because a negation cannot
-- be a sole binder — and that sentence is true about BINDING and false about
-- CONNECTEDNESS. `coverage_gap(I, Con) :- pack(I, ...), concern(..., Con), not
-- concern_covered(I, Con).` has its two positive premises joined THROUGH the
-- negation, and with the negation invisible they read as disconnected and both
-- head variables reported as passengers. The sweep returned 103 passengers,
-- most of them that shape.
--
-- So the two questions need two relations. `binds` is positive only — that is
-- what binding MEANS. `mentions` is both, because sharing a variable with a
-- negated premise is still sharing.
neg_args(R, K, Args) :- premise_lit(R, K, $not($lit(_, _, Args, _))).
neg_cell(R, K, L)    :- neg_args(R, K, L).
neg_cell(R, K, T)    :- neg_cell(R, K, $cons(_, T)).
neg_var(R, K, V)     :- neg_cell(R, K, $cons($var(V), _)).
neg_var(R, K, V)     :- premise_lit(R, K, $not($lit(_, $var(V), _, _))).

-- AND A BUILTIN PREMISE JOINS TWO PREMISES WITHOUT BINDING EITHER. This was
-- written down as a KNOWN HOLE on 2026-09-10 and deliberately left open —
-- "the `$not` one was assumed safe and was not, so this one gets measured
-- before it gets fixed". Measured now: the operands of `$builtin(Op, Args)`
-- are a `$cons` list in exactly the shape `$lit` uses, so the same three-line
-- walk opens it. `being_fed(F, Pct) :- fed_share(F, Pct), fed_cut(C), Pct >=
-- C.` was the case that named the hole — two premises reading as disconnected
-- although the comparison joins them, and `Pct` reported as a passenger it is
-- not.
--
-- A BUILTIN JOINS AND NEVER CARRIES, and the split is the whole repair. A
-- comparison binds nothing, so it can never be a passenger's sole binder and
-- can never be `disconnected` itself; it can only put two premises in touch.
-- So the question splits in two and the two feed different consumers:
--
--   mentions   positive and negated premises — what the rule is ABOUT, and
--              therefore what "this body has more than one premise" counts
--   joins      those plus builtin operands — what makes two premises touch
--
-- MEASURED WHEN THEY WERE ONE RELATION, which is why they are two: feeding
-- builtins into `mentions` killed the three rows the hole was named for and
-- OPENED NINE NEW ONES. `test_file(T) :- artifact(T, ts), D is str_pre(T, "/"),
-- D = "test".` has ONE positive premise and nothing to stand apart from, and
-- the two builtins pushed it over `multi_premise` — so a rule that was
-- correctly silent became a passenger. Net minus three, and thirteen rows
-- wrong in both directions.
--
-- THE NESTING LIMIT IS WHY, and it is the same one the head walk has. `D is
-- str_pre(T, "/")` reifies its right operand as the compound `str_pre($var(
-- "T"), "/")`, so this walk sees `$var("D")` on the spine and never sees T.
-- With builtins counted as premises that is a MISSED join on a rule that has
-- been made to look joinable — the two errors compound. With them counted as
-- joins only, a missed mention can at worst leave a premise reading
-- disconnected on a rule that already had two real premises, which
-- over-reports rather than hides.
bi_args(R, K, Args)  :- premise_lit(R, K, $builtin(_, Args)).
bi_cell(R, K, L)     :- bi_args(R, K, L).
bi_cell(R, K, T)     :- bi_cell(R, K, $cons(_, T)).
bi_var(R, K, V)      :- bi_cell(R, K, $cons($var(V), _)).

mentions(R, K, V)    :- body_var(R, K, V).
mentions(R, K, V)    :- neg_var(R, K, V).

joins(R, K, V)       :- mentions(R, K, V).
joins(R, K, V)       :- bi_var(R, K, V).

-- ============================================ SHAPE 1: A CONSTANT SUBJECT
-- The head has no variables at all. That alone is not enough: `sealed(rules)`,
-- `sealed(assertions)` and `sealed(provenance)` are three propositions and the
-- argument is what tells them apart. The argument carries information exactly
-- when the relation takes MORE THAN ONE shape — so the discriminator is
-- whether ANY rule concluding that relation has a variable head, and it is
-- derived rather than assumed.
-- A HEAD THAT IS ALREADY NULLARY IS THE DESTINATION, NOT A CANDIDATE, and the
-- first demo caught this the moment nullary heads existed: `deploy_blocked()`
-- has `$nil` for its arguments, therefore no variables, therefore read as a
-- constant head — the model recommending the thing it was looking at. The
-- head must have at least one argument for the question to mean anything, and
-- `$cons(_, _)` is how a list says it is not empty.
-- A VARIABLE NESTED INSIDE A HEAD TERM IS ONE THIS MODEL CANNOT SEE, and the
-- limit is the language's rather than this file's. `forged[audit]($fact(R, P,
-- A))` names three variables and `head_cell` walks the `$cons` SPINE only, so
-- the top of that cell is `$fact(...)` and not `$var(...)` — the head read as
-- constant and `forged` was reported as a proposition it is not. There is no
-- generic term destructor here (measured: `str_len` refuses an atom, a number
-- and a compound alike, so it is not an "is this atomic" test), and safety.rofl
-- says why in as many words — "a term carries an arbitrary functor and Datalog
-- cannot destructure one it does not name", which is exactly why the kernel
-- seeds `premise_var` from the host instead.
--
-- So the functor is NAMED, the way safety.rofl names `$lit`, `$not` and
-- `$builtin`: `$fact` is the kernel's own reified fact and part of the same
-- vocabulary. THE RESIDUAL IS STATED AND CANNOT BE DERIVED — a USER functor in
-- a head (`p($pair(X, Y)) :- ...`) is still invisible and would still read as
-- constant, and there is no rule that can find it for the reason above. It has
-- no audit row because an audit row would be a lie about its own coverage;
-- what it has instead is a NUMBER the scanner measures textually and prints
-- beside the verdict, so the size of the blind spot is on the report.
head_var(R, V)      :- head_cell(R, $cons($fact($var(V), _, _), _)).
head_var(R, V)      :- head_cell(R, $cons($fact(_, $var(V), _), _)).
head_var(R, V)      :- head_cell(R, $cons($fact(_, _, $var(V)), _)).

const_head(R)       :- head_args(R, $cons(_, _)), not head_var(R, _).
varying_head(Rel)   :- concludes(R, Rel), head_var(R, _).

-- TWO CONSTANT TUPLES ARE A DISCRIMINATING ARGUMENT. `not varying_head` asks
-- whether any rule writes a VARIABLE there and that is only half the question:
-- `slow_because(fixpoint, barren_rules)`, `slow_because(fixpoint, late_filter)`
-- and `slow_because(fixpoint, re_derivation)` are three constant heads, no
-- variable among them, and the second argument is the entire content of the
-- relation. A proposition has ONE row; a relation with two distinct head
-- shapes is a table.
multi_shape(Rel)    :- concludes(P, Rel), head_args(P, A),
                       concludes(Q, Rel), head_args(Q, B), A != B.

-- AND A CONSUMER THAT BINDS THE ARGUMENT IS READING IT. `saves_nothing(
-- reuse_plan)` has one constant head and no twin, and `pure_overhead(P) :-
-- saves_nothing(P), overhead_exceeds_work(P).` carries `reuse_plan` out
-- through P into `slow_because(parse, reuse_plan)` — the constant is the
-- answer's NAME, not a dress. A dummy argument is one nothing ever binds.
read_as_var(Rel)    :- premise_lit(R, K, $lit(Rel, _, _, _)), body_var(R, K, _).
read_as_var(Rel)    :- premise_lit(R, K, $not($lit(Rel, _, _, _))), neg_var(R, K, _).

dressed_constant[audit](Rel) :- const_head(R), concludes(R, Rel),
                                not varying_head(Rel), not multi_shape(Rel),
                                not read_as_var(Rel).

-- ============================================ SHAPE 2: A PASSENGER SUBJECT
-- The head carries a VARIABLE the body binds only so there is something to
-- name. `any_unblocker(W) :- takeable(W), unblocking_work(_).` — W is in the
-- head, `takeable(W)` is its only binder, and that premise shares nothing with
-- the rest of the body. Drop both and the rule is a proposition.
--
-- THIS IS THE SAME SET `rule_shape` REPORTS AS A CROSS PRODUCT, from the other
-- side: a disconnected premise that is the sole binder of the head's variables
-- is not an accident of join order, it is a head that wanted to be nullary.
occurs_in(R, V, K)  :- body_var(R, K, V).
elsewhere(R, V, K)  :- occurs_in(R, V, J), occurs_in(R, V, K), J != K.
sole_binder(R, V, K) :- head_var(R, V), occurs_in(R, V, K), not elsewhere(R, V, K).

shares(R, K, J)     :- joins(R, K, V), joins(R, J, V), K != J.
connected(R, K)     :- shares(R, K, _).
disconnected(R, K)  :- body_var(R, K, _), not connected(R, K).

-- A ONE-PREMISE BODY IS ALWAYS `disconnected` AND THAT IS NOT A PASSENGER.
-- The first run reported 28 of them — every recursive walk in this very file
-- among them, because `head_cell(R, T) :- head_cell(R, $cons(_, T))` has one
-- premise and nothing to be apart FROM. A passenger needs a body it stands
-- apart from, so the rule must have two premises before the question means
-- anything. Caught by the positive control on the first run; without it the
-- audit would have been 28 rows of noise around 2 real ones.
multi_premise(R)       :- mentions(R, K, _), mentions(R, J, _), K != J.

-- AND A BODY EVERY PREMISE OF WHICH FEEDS THE HEAD IS A PRODUCT ON PURPOSE.
-- `cell[audit](Lang, K, L) :- node_kind(Lang, K), layer(L).` is the matrix of
-- the JS model: two disconnected premises, three head variables, and the head
-- IS the product — every one of the three is read downstream and none can be
-- dropped. Without this premise the audit called all three passengers and
-- recommended emptying a head that is the whole point of the relation.
--
-- So a passenger needs something to be a passenger ON: a POSITIVE premise that
-- puts nothing into the head, which is a cross-product factor asking an
-- existential question. `unblocking_work(_)` and `ast_node[code](_, _, _, _)`
-- are the shape; `layer(L)` is not.
--
-- POSITIVE ONLY, and the negative case is why the restriction is written
-- rather than left implicit: range restriction means a NEGATED premise can
-- never feed the head, so counting negations here would make `p(X) :- q(X),
-- not r(_).` a passenger rule — and X is that relation's subject.
feeds_head(R, K)       :- body_var(R, K, V), head_var(R, V).
guard_premise(R, K)    :- body_args(R, K, _), not feeds_head(R, K).
has_guard(R)           :- guard_premise(R, _).

passenger[audit](R, V) :- sole_binder(R, V, K), disconnected(R, K),
                          multi_premise(R), has_guard(R).

-- ============================================================ THE VERDICT
--
-- ONE PASSENGER IS NOT A NULLARY HEAD. The first version of this line fired on
-- ANY passenger, and a head with two variables of which one is a passenger is
-- a head with one variable — not a proposition. `share(R, P) :- touched(R, T),
-- total_width(A), P is T * 100 / A.` is the case that measured it: `R` reads
-- as a passenger (falsely, see the nesting limit above — `T` hides inside the
-- arithmetic), `P` does not, and the rule was reported as wanting a nullary
-- head when dropping `R` at most narrows it. You may drop the head only when
-- EVERY variable in it is droppable.
--
-- AND IT IS THE CHEAPEST GUARD AGAINST THE NESTING LIMIT ON THIS TREE: a false
-- passenger has to be the rule's ONLY head variable to reach the verdict now,
-- so the arithmetic blind spot costs a `passenger` row and not a work item.
head_has_stayer(R)  :- head_var(R, V), not passenger[audit](R, V).
all_passengers(R)   :- passenger[audit](R, _), not head_has_stayer(R).

wants_nullary[audit](Rel) :- dressed_constant[audit](Rel).
wants_nullary[audit](Rel) :- all_passengers(R), concludes(R, Rel).

-- WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE:
--   * A head variable bound by a premise that IS connected but whose
--     connection is incidental — `p(X) :- q(X, Y), r(Y).` X may still be a
--     passenger, and "nothing downstream reads X" is a fact about the CALLERS
--     that one rule's reflection cannot hold.
--   * WHETHER THE PROPOSITION SHOULD EXIST. A rule wanting a nullary head
--     might instead want inlining into its one caller. That is a judgement
--     about the program, and the row is where somebody makes it.
--   * A VARIABLE INSIDE AN ARITHMETIC OPERAND, and this one can never be
--     fixed. `P is T * 100 / A` reifies its right operand as a compound and
--     the walk sees the `$cons` spine only, so `T` never appears and its
--     premise reads as disconnected from a comparison that in fact uses it.
--     The seven string destructors ARE writable as term patterns (`str_pre(
--     $var(V), _)` matches) and could be named the way `$fact` is; an
--     OPERATOR functor is not — `*(A, B)` is refused with `expected a term,
--     got '*'`. A hole closed for seven cases and open for arithmetic reads
--     as closed, which is worse than one written down. What it costs is
--     bounded: a false `passenger` row, which cannot reach the verdict unless
--     it is the rule's only head variable.
--   * A VARIABLE INSIDE A USER FUNCTOR IN A HEAD, for the reason above the
--     `$fact` clauses. Counted rather than assumed: the report prints how many
--     `const_head` rules hold a `$var` somewhere inside a term this file
--     cannot open, so the blind spot has a number every run.
--
-- THE BUILTIN HOLE WAS THE THIRD ITEM HERE UNTIL 2026-09-10 and is repaired
-- above. It read: "`premise_lit` carries `$builtin(Op, ...)` and this file does
-- not open it... NOT repaired here, because it needs the operand terms walked
-- and the fix should be measured rather than assumed — the `$not` one was
-- assumed safe and was not." It was measured, and the measurement earned its
-- caution: the obvious repair killed three rows and opened nine.
